 your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': "**Hand Analysis: Betting for Value and Protection**\n\nOkay, so I'm in hand number two of this session, down a hundred chips already. We're playing heads-up, and it seems the blinds are in play with the usual small pot action. The board is pretty dynamic: King-high, with a Ten, Eight, and Ace. I've got King-Seven offsuit. My initial instinct is that I have top pair with a good kicker and that the opponent, based on their preflop, flop, and turn checks, is likely weak. They have shown zero aggression on this board which gives me a solid read that I'm probably ahead. I can't let a flush or straight draw get a free card, so I need to bet. The current pot is small, which indicates I need to make a move. Given a bet size is defined at 100, I'll go for that to get value from worse hands (10x, 8x, pocket pairs < K) and make the draws pay to see the river. My reasoning is that the Ace on the turn is a scare card, meaning the opponent will not want to bet with an Ace. My hand is currently K-K-A-10-8. Their passivity is a clear tell. I need to protect my top pair, extract value, and deny equity. Betting 100 seems like the right play.\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: The opponent has been extremely passive, checking pre-flop, on the flop, and now on the turn. You hold a pair of Kings (Top Pair) with the board K-10-8-A. While your kicker (7) is weak and currently counterfeited by the board (your hand is K-K-A-10-8), you are likely ahead of the opponent's range, which seems to lack an Ace or strong King given the checks. Checking back gives a free card to potential flush or straight draws. A small bet extracts value from worse pairs (like 10x or 8x) and protects your hand.\n\nReason: You likely have the best hand with Top Pair. The opponent's passivity suggests weakness. Betting gains value and protection against draws.\n\nAnswer: 100"}
