ximum value, by letting me keep betting.\n*   Betting has very thin value here, and a check will allow me to realize some value by seeing the opponent\'s holdings.\n*   If I bet, I might get called by worse hands, but I could get check-raised by better ones, especially straights. This makes me vulnerable.\n*   Checking the River is the safest play. There is a higher probability that I chop against a King, or win against a lower pair, or a missed draw.\n\n**Therefore, my decision is to check.**  I\'m happy to realize my equity and get to showdown cheaply, especially on this kind of board. The risk-reward is heavily tilted towards checking in this scenario. I don\'t want to get bluffed or trap myself into a bigger loss if I bet. It\'s a textbook "value-extraction" spot with a check.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation:\nThe current board is King, 10, 8, Ace, Queen. This is a very "wet" board with four broadway cards. Specifically, any Jack makes a Broadway Straight (10, J, Q, K, A). Additionally, there is an Ace on the board, meaning any random Ace in the opponent\'s hand beats your pair of Kings. Your hand is a Pair of Kings with an Ace-Queen-10 kicker (the board plays for your kickers). You are beating middle pairs (Queens, Tens) or missed draws, but you are losing to any Ace, any two pair, sets, or a Straight. The opponent has shown passivity (checking all streets, calling the turn), which suggests they might have a marginal hand or a draw. Betting here is risky because better hands (Ace, Straight) will call or raise, while worse hands (Queens, Tens) will likely fold given the scary board. There is very little value in betting.\n\nReason:\nYou have a Pair of Kings, which is a decent showdown hand but vulnerable on this specific board (A, K, Q, 10, 8). The presence of the Ace and the straight possibility (Jack) makes betting dangerous. Checking allows you to get to showdown cheaply to see if you win against a weaker pair or chop with another King, without risking more chips against a superior hand.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
